// Word data the T9 game draws from: English words, length 4-8.
//
// Built in layers:
// 1. Start from a broad dictionary word list (words_alpha), filtered to
//    words that are real per WordNet's lemma list (excluding WordNet's
//    'instance' senses -- specific named people/places like "Dumas" or
//    "Orion" -- so those are excluded while dual-use words like "hammer" or
//    "Iranian" are kept; a synset can carry both an instance pointer and a
//    separate class pointer at once, e.g. Orion's constellation sense, so
//    any instance pointer marks it as a name regardless of what else is on
//    the same synset) or a regular inflection of one (stripping a regular
//    -s/-es/-ed/-ing/-er/-est suffix, handling e-drop and doubled
//    consonants, and restricting each suffix to the WordNet parts of speech
//    it can plausibly inflect from -- e.g. -ing/-ed only match a verb sense,
//    -ies only a noun sense -- so a word that only exists in WordNet as a
//    noun can't be credited as real just because stripping -ed off it
//    happens to leave that noun spelled out, as happened with "nimmed"
//    passing via the noun "nim"). WordNet and ENABLE (a Scrabble word list)
//    are applied right after each other here as two separate, independent
//    filters -- not merged into one signal: a direct WordNet lemma match is
//    kept outright, while a regular-inflection match additionally has to
//    have its exact surface spelling confirmed present in ENABLE, to catch
//    cases where the inflection-stripping guessed wrong (e.g. "chairer",
//    "betterer" look like regular agent-noun/comparative forms but aren't
//    real words). Plus a small hand-added list of function words and
//    irregular verb/plural forms WordNet does not index at all (else,
//    could, went, were, women, isle, fled, ...), carried forward from the
//    original build.
// 2. Keep only words where the word ITSELF, or the base it stems from, is
//    common enough to appear in the top 100,000 of a frequency-ranked word
//    list of 1.66 million English words (hermitdave/FrequencyWords'
//    en_full.txt, built from the same OpenSubtitles corpus as the smaller
//    50k list used originally) -- e.g. "beget" passes at 100k (rank 40,751)
//    despite being far too rare for the original 30k cutoff against the
//    smaller list, and "saltine"/"tonal" likewise. Deliberately looser than
//    gating on the inflected surface form directly (which rejected
//    legitimate words like "lamer"), tightened from an initial 50k cutoff
//    to control obscurity/difficulty, loosened from 20k to 30k after 20k
//    rejected "repel" despite it being a common word, then widened to a
//    100k cutoff against the much larger en_full.txt once the 50k-word list
//    itself turned out to be the real bottleneck keeping out thousands of
//    otherwise-ordinary words.
// 3. A manual pass over every word step 1-2 added to WORDS_GOOD, reading
//    each one's actual WordNet definition, removing ones that looked
//    wrong -- double inflections ("aboded"), misspellings ("chaped" for
//    "chapped"), nonstandard comparatives ("wronger"), overly obscure/
//    archaic/dialectal terms ("bedlamer", "gentles"), proper nouns/ethnic
//    group names used as generic plurals ("aztecs", "mongols"), and
//    offensive terms (slurs and other inappropriate words, e.g. "dagos",
//    "injun", "squaw") -- plus a lighter pattern-based safety scan (not an
//    exhaustive manual review) over the much larger set of newly-added
//    real-but-not-good words each time the cutoff widened or the source
//    dictionary grew, catching things like "fakeer", proper-noun/
//    organization leaks the name heuristic can't catch because they aren't
//    personal names ("haganah", "tammany", "purim"), and further slurs and
//    offensive terms (racial, ethnic, and ableist) including ones hidden
//    among real words sharing a substring with a slur root (keeping
//    "aspic"/"gypsum"/"coonskin"/"dyke"(embankment)/"cracker"(food)/
//    "paddy"(rice) whose dominant sense is unrelated and benign, while
//    removing "spic"/"gypped"/"redskins"/"darkie"/"mulatto" and similar
//    whose only real meaning is the slur). See exclude_good.txt /
//    exclude_dict_only.txt in the scratchpad this was built from for the
//    exact lists (both get removed from DICTIONARY; exclude_good.txt
//    additionally guarantees removal from WORDS_GOOD specifically).
//    Archaic and highly technical words are deliberately NOT filtered out
//    beyond this -- WordNet's domain/usage tags turned out to have poor
//    recall and a high false-positive rate (flagging ordinary words like
//    "email" or "median" as technical purely because WordNet files their
//    sense under a specialist subject heading), so real words of this kind
//    are allowed through rather than built against an unreliable filter.
// 4. A small hand-added list of words missing from words_alpha itself (so
//    they never reached step 1 at all) despite being legitimate WordNet
//    lemmas or regular inflections confirmed present in ENABLE -- e.g.
//    "spay"/"spayed"/"spays"/"spaying" and "tonal", found when a player
//    asked why they weren't recognized (both now also pass the normal
//    frequency gate on their own at the 100k cutoff, making the manual add
//    redundant for these specific words, but harmless to leave in place).
//    See added_words.txt in the scratchpad for the exact list.
//
// DICTIONARY: the full list, used to check whether a typed guess is a real
// word at all (hangman mode).
// WORDS_GOOD: the subset that is also 'good' under the current t9-logic.js
// rules (including wraparound merges, e.g. HINDU), precomputed offline --
// classifying the whole dictionary against every possible circular
// digit-merge takes several seconds, too slow to redo on every page load --
// rather than at runtime. Whenever the good/bad rules in t9-logic.js
// change, or the dictionary itself is regenerated, this needs regenerating
// to match -- see the isGoodWord-based filter used to build it.
// WORDS_GOOD_RANK[i] is WORDS_GOOD[i]'s frequency rank (1 = most common,
// out of the fuller 1.66-million-word en_full.txt frequency list, kept for
// finer-grained difficulty gauging even though the gate above only checks
// the top 100k) -- its own rank if it has one, else its base stem's rank.
// Used to gauge a hangman puzzle's difficulty from how rare its rarest
// valid answer is.
